    <info>
        <title>Reconciliation Service API</title>
        <author>
            <orgname>TEI Publisher Community</orgname>
        </author>
        <abstract>
            <para>Turn your TEI Publisher app into an OpenRefine-style reconciliation service,
                matching external data against your own registers.</para>
        </abstract>
        <keywordset role="genre">
            <keyword>Documentation</keyword>
        </keywordset>
    </info>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-overview">
        <title>Overview</title>
        <para>The <emphasis>reconcile</emphasis> profile turns a TEI Publisher application into a
            server implementing the <link
                xlink:href="https://reconciliation-api.github.io/specs/">W3C Community Group
                Reconciliation Service API</link>, the same protocol used by the <link
                xlink:href="annotations.xml">Reconciliation Service connector</link>
            described in the annotation editor documentation. Once enabled, other tools — OpenRefine,
            the reconciliation-api <link xlink:href="https://reconciliation-api.github.io/testbench/"
                >testbench</link>, or another TEI Publisher instance's own Reconciliation Service
            connector — can query your registers of people, places, organizations and works to find
            matching entities, retrieve additional properties, or fetch an HTML preview.</para>
        <para>Both API versions currently in use are supported from the same endpoint: the mature
                <emphasis>0.2</emphasis> release and the upcoming <emphasis>1.0-draft</emphasis>. A
            client selects a version with a <code>?version=0.2</code> query parameter on the
            manifest request (the default, parameter-less manifest is 1.0-draft); the reconcile and
            data-extension endpoints detect the version automatically from the shape of the request
            body, so no separate configuration is needed for either protocol.</para>
        <para>The server and the client-side connector are deliberately <emphasis>decoupled</emphasis>:
            the Reconciliation Service connector does not depend on this profile and can point at any
            conforming reconciliation service, and this profile's endpoint can equally be queried by
            any conforming client, not just TEI Publisher's own. You do not need the <code
                >annotate</code> profile to run a reconciliation server, and you do not need this
            profile to use the Reconciliation Service connector against someone else's server.</para>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-adding">
        <title>Adding the profile to an application</title>
        <para>Add <code>reconcile</code> to your application's <code>extends</code> list in
                <filename>config.json</filename> — it only depends on <code>base10</code>:</para>
        <programlisting language="json" xml:space="preserve"><![CDATA[
{
    "extends": ["base10", "reconcile"]
}
]]></programlisting>
        <para>The profile registers its Open API routes under the prefix <option>reconc</option>,
            mounted at <code>/api/reconcile</code> (see <link xlink:href="documentation.xml"
                >Extending the API</link> for how this mechanism works in general). No further
            configuration is required to get started — the defaults described below cover the
            typical case of reconciling against person, place, organization and work registers.
            The <emphasis>registers</emphasis> profile (the one that adds browse pages like
                <filename>/people</filename>) is entirely optional: if it happens to be part of
            your application too, <code>GET /api/reconcile/entity/{id}</code> automatically
            redirects there; if not, the same endpoint falls back to a built-in preview
            transparently — see <link linkend="reconcile-config">Customizing what gets
                reconciled</link> below.</para>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-endpoints">
        <title>Endpoints</title>
        <para>All endpoints mandated by the specification are implemented for both versions, plus
            the optional services recommended for a fully-featured service:</para>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>GET /api/reconcile</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Service manifest. Add <code>?version=0.2</code> for a 0.2-shaped manifest;
                        omit it for 1.0-draft (the default).</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>POST /api/reconcile (also /api/reconcile/match)</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Batch reconciliation queries. Accepts both JSON (1.0-draft) and the
                        classic <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> <code>queries=&lt;json&gt;</code>
                        convention (0.2), detected automatically.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>GET /api/reconcile/suggest/entity, /suggest/property, /suggest/type</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Auto-suggest services used by OpenRefine's search-as-you-type UI.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>GET /api/reconcile/preview</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>HTML preview fragment for a single entity, shown by OpenRefine in a
                        popover.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>GET /api/reconcile/entity/{id}</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Redirects to the entity's normal registers browse page when its type
                        defines one <emphasis>and</emphasis> the registers profile is actually
                        installed, otherwise falls back to the same preview rendering.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>POST /api/reconcile/extend, GET /extend/propose</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Data-extension services: fetch additional property values for a set of
                        entities, and propose the catalog of extendable properties for a type. The
                        classic <code>GET /api/reconcile?extend=&lt;json&gt;</code> convention is
                        also supported.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-config">
        <title>Customizing what gets reconciled</title>
        <para>By default, the profile reconciles against four types drawn from the app's
                <filename>registers</filename> collection — <emphasis>person</emphasis>,
                <emphasis>place</emphasis>, <emphasis>organization</emphasis> and <emphasis>work</emphasis>
            — the same collection the entity editors in the <emphasis>annotate</emphasis> profile
            create and edit (this is a base10-level convention; the separate <emphasis
                >registers</emphasis> profile that adds browse pages over that same collection is
            not required, see below). This is entirely configurable: what counts as an entity of a given
            type, how its label and properties are extracted, how candidates are scored and ranked,
            and how a preview is rendered are all defined in one plain, editable module,
                <filename>modules/reconcile-config.xql</filename>, kept separate from the HTTP
            handling code so it can be safely customized without touching the profile's routes.</para>
        <para>Like other user-editable profile modules (for example <filename
                >profiles/annotate/modules/annotations/tei-annotation-config.tpl.xqm</filename>),
                <filename>reconcile-config.xql</filename> is copied into a new application by <code
                >jinks create</code> and left alone on subsequent <code>jinks update</code> runs — if
            you have edited it, Jinks reports a conflict rather than silently overwriting your
            changes.</para>
        <para>Each entry in <code>$reconc-config:TYPES</code> is a map describing one reconcilable
            type:</para>
        <programlisting language="xquery" xml:space="preserve"><![CDATA[
"person": map {
    "name": "Person",                    (: display name, manifest + suggest/type :)
    "view": "people",                    (: optional: registers browse-page name for /entity/{id} :)
    "entities": function() as element()* {
        collection($config:register-root)/id($config:register-map?person?id)//tei:person[@xml:id]
    },
    "label": function($e as element()) as xs:string { ... },  (: OR an xs:string XPath :)
    "preview-mode": "register-overview", (: optional: ODD web-transform mode for the default preview :)
    "properties": map {
        "gender": map { "name": "Gender", "value": function($e as element()) as xs:string* { ... } }
    }
}
]]></programlisting>
        <para>The <option>label</option> field and each property's <option>value</option> field
            accept either a compiled function item, or an <code>xs:string</code> treated as a
            relative XPath expression evaluated dynamically against the matched entity — e.g.
                <code>".//tei:persName[@type='main'][1]"</code>. Function items are what the shipped
            defaults use and are the safer, faster choice for anything beyond a single path
            expression; XPath strings are quicker to write for straightforward cases. The
                <option>entities</option> field selecting all candidates for a type is always a
            function item, since the context for a bare selection XPath (which collection? relative
            to what?) is inherently ambiguous.</para>
        <para>Matching and ranking is likewise pluggable: <code>$reconc-config:SCORE</code> points at
            any function <code>function($label as xs:string?, $query as xs:string?) as xs:double</code>,
            defaulting to a string-similarity algorithm in <filename
                >modules/reconcile-scoring.xql</filename> that you may reuse, wrap, or fully replace
            — for example to call an external NER or embedding-based similarity service. The default
            already tolerates typos (a Levenshtein-based fallback tier, applied word by word so one
            mistyped word in a multi-word name doesn't sink the whole match), and a type may also
            list every name <emphasis>variant</emphasis> worth matching against — not just its
            preferred display name — via an optional <option>labels</option> field. For larger
            collections, a type can additionally supply a <option>fulltext-search</option> function
            that pre-filters candidates through a Lucene full-text index (with the same typo
            tolerance) before the precise scoring step runs, rather than scoring every entity
            individually. Adding a new type, removing one, or pointing an existing type at a
            completely different collection requires no change anywhere else in the profile: the
            manifest, suggest services, preview and data-extension endpoints all derive automatically
            from <code>$reconc-config:TYPES</code>.</para>
        <para>A reconciliation query isn't limited to a plain name either: both spec versions let a
            client add <emphasis>property</emphasis> conditions (e.g. "name is Goethe <emphasis
                >and</emphasis> gender is male") to disambiguate same-named candidates, and 1.0-draft
            also allows matching directly by a known entity <emphasis>id</emphasis> — a query may
            even carry no name condition at all, reconciling purely by a known property value. No
            extra configuration is needed for this: every property already defined for <code
                >/extend</code> doubles as a match condition input. See <filename
                >profiles/reconcile/doc/README.md</filename>'s "Matching on more than just a name"
            section for the full <option>required</option>/<option>matchQualifier</option>/<option
                >matchQuantifier</option> rules.</para>
        <para>Preview rendering (used by <code>/preview</code> and the <code>/entity/{id}</code>
            fallback) reuses the application's own TEI Processing Model transformation by default —
            the same mechanism the registers browse pages use — in the type's
                <option>preview-mode</option> (default <code>register-overview</code>). Set a
            different existing ODD mode, or supply a <option>preview</option> function item for full
            control over rendering for that type.</para>
        <para>The <option>view</option> field (e.g. <code>"view": "people"</code> on the shipped
                <emphasis>person</emphasis> type) names a <emphasis>registers</emphasis> profile
            browse page to redirect <code>/entity/{id}</code> to — but only if that profile is
            actually installed in this app, checked at request time rather than assumed. Without
            it (or with no <option>view</option> at all), the default preview rendering above is
            used instead. For full control, set <option>view</option> to a function item instead
            of a string — <code>function($id, $found, $request) as item()*</code>, building the
            response yourself.</para>
        <para>The full reference, including a worked example of adding a fifth type, ships with the
            profile source at <filename>profiles/reconcile/doc/README.md</filename>.</para>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-production">
        <title>Deploying to production</title>
        <para>The profile ships with no request-rate limit and no authentication requirement by
            default — matching how real-world reconciliation services (Wikidata's, GND's, etc.) are
            conventionally deployed, and the spec itself defines no auth mechanism. Batch requests
            and <code>/extend</code> calls are capped by a handful of overridable
                <filename>reconcile-config.xql</filename> limits (rejecting anything oversized with
            HTTP 400, since a batch response's positional shape can't tolerate silent truncation;
            an over-large <code>limit</code> parameter is clamped instead of rejected). Rate limiting
            is deliberately left to a reverse proxy in front of the app rather than hand-rolled in
            XQuery, and restricting access to specific operations is a matter of adding an <code
                >x-constraints</code> object to them in <filename>reconcile-api.tpl.json</filename> —
            the <code>basicAuth</code>/<code>cookieAuth</code> schemes needed for that are already
            declared. See <filename>profiles/reconcile/doc/README.md</filename>'s "Production
            hardening" section for the exact config variables and worked examples.</para>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="reconcile-client-side">
        <title>Querying a reconciliation service from TEI Publisher</title>
        <para>To reconcile entities <emphasis>against</emphasis> a service — this one, or any other
            conforming 0.2 or 1.0-draft service — from within the annotation editor, use the
                <link xlink:href="annotations.xml">Reconciliation Service
                connector</link>, which can also be nested inside the Custom connector to combine
            external matches with a local TEI register.</para>
    </section>
</article>
